    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A growing body of research suggests that balancing selection is an important evolutionary force acting in natural populations. Balancing selection typically occurs when conditionally beneficial alleles are maintained in a population via varying spatial or temporal selection pressures. One species that exhibits balancing selection is Drosophila melanogaster, which rapidly evolves in response to seasonal fluctuations among seasons. Previous work has shown that seasonal adaptation causes frequency oscillations in approximately 2000 single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) and phenotypic traits (e.g. metabolic and reproduction profiles) across winter and summer seasons. We hypothesize that variation at cis-regulatory regions (cis-eQTL), regions regulating mRNA expression, contributes to rapid and cyclic seasonal adaptation via modulation of environment-dependent gene expression. To test this hypothesis, we used two sequencing methods on winter-summer hybrid Drosophila populations that were subjected to either winter-like or summer-like conditions. The first technique, RNA-Seq, is a method that creates gene expression profiles and the second, FAIRE-Seq, is a process that isolates and enriches openly-transcribed chromatin through phenol-chloroform separation. While FAIRE-Seq has been used successfully in the past, this project elucidates the various means by which the protocol must be trouble-shot and adjusted for individual experiments. Consideration of other chromatin sequencing methods, such as ATAC-Seq and DNase-Seq, may be more practical for future experiments involving open-chromatin research.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
